{
  "id": "proj-brick-barbecue",
  "slug": "brick-barbecue",
  "status": "published",
  "title": "Brick Barbecue",
  "summary": "A freestanding brick grill with a cooking grate and wood store. Mortar, levels and patience.",
  "difficulty": "advanced",
  "estimatedHours": 20,
  "imageUrl": null,
  "currency": "USD",
  "steps": [
    "Mark a 5 ft x 3 ft slab, dig it out 8 in deep, lay and compact 4 in of gravel, and set level 2x4 forms on stakes. Lay rebar in a 12 in grid tied with wire and lifted 2 in off the gravel on brick offcuts.",
    "Mix and pour the slab from 80 lb bags of concrete, screed it level with the forms and float it. Keep it damp under plastic sheeting for 7 days before building on it.",
    "Dry-lay the first course in a U (back wall and two sides) plus a centre divider that makes the wood store, adjusting the joints so the courses use whole and half bricks. Mark the lines on the slab.",
    "Lay the walls in Type S mortar with 3/8 in joints in running bond. Check level, plumb and the mason's line on every course, and tool the joints with the jointer when they are thumbprint-hard.",
    "At about 28 in (charcoal tray) and 34 in (cooking grate), turn a brick header-out into the inner face of each side wall every 18 in to make corbels the tray and grate rest on.",
    "Line the floor and back of the fire chamber with firebrick set in refractory mortar so ordinary brick never takes direct flame.",
    "Finish with a cap course of bricks on edge, brush off mortar smears, and cover the work for a week. Start with small fires and build to full heat over several cooks."
  ],
